{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"5b6004ad-d923-46ea-bd62-8c5feef0d2f1","title":"Snell's law: light bending at a boundary"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5b6004ad-d923-46ea-bd62-8c5feef0d2f1","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Light changes direction when it crosses between materials. The normal is perpendicular to the boundary; refractive index describes each material.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Snell's law"},{"op":"figure","id":"boundary_fig","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3.8,3.8],"y":[-3.5,3.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"boundary","from":[-3.6,0],"to":[3.6,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"crossing","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"normal","from":[0,-3.1],"to":[0,3.1],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.7,3.1],"text":"normal","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.3,1.7],"text":"air: n₁ = 1.00"},{"kind":"text","at":[-2,-1.8],"text":"glass: n₂ = 1.50"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5b6004ad-d923-46ea-bd62-8c5feef0d2f1","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The incoming ray makes a forty-five-degree angle with the normal. Always measure these angles from the normal, not the surface.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"incident_ink","on":"boundary_fig","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"incident","from":[-2.8,2.8],"to":{"at":"crossing"},"arrow":true},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"crossing"},"from":[0,3],"to":[-2.8,2.8],"r":0.2,"label":"θ₁ = 45°"}]},{"op":"text","id":"angle_note","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Measure angles from the normal"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5b6004ad-d923-46ea-bd62-8c5feef0d2f1","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Snell's law connects both angles and refractive indices. Entering higher-index glass bends the transmitted ray toward the normal.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"transmit_ink","on":"boundary_fig","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"transmitted","from":{"at":"crossing"},"to":[1.496663,-2.8],"arrow":true}]},{"op":"equation","id":"snell","anchor":{"el":"angle_note","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"n_1\\sin\\theta_1=n_2\\sin\\theta_2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"5b6004ad-d923-46ea-bd62-8c5feef0d2f1","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Substituting gives a refracted angle of about twenty-eight degrees, smaller than forty-five degrees. The higher-index material brings the ray closer to the normal.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"calculation","anchor":{"el":"snell","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\sin\\theta_2=\\frac{1.00}{1.50}\\sin45^\\circ\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\theta_2\\approx28.1^\\circ"},{"op":"figure","id":"refract_angle","on":"boundary_fig","marks":[{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"crossing"},"from":[0,-3],"to":{"on":"transmitted","t":1},"r":0.24,"label":"θ₂ = 28.1°"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"When light enters a higher-index material, does it bend toward or away from the normal?","expected_gist":"Toward the normal; its angle with the normal decreases."}}}
